      "topic_id": "algebraic_proof",
      "prompt": "Prove algebraically that the sum of the squares of two consecutive integers is always odd.",
      "solution_steps": [
        "Let two consecutive integers be n and n + 1.",
        "Write the given expression algebraically: n^2 + (n + 1)^2.",
        "Expand and simplify: n^2 + (n + 1)^2 = 2n^2 + 2n + 1.",
        "Factorise/rearrange to expose the structure: 2n^2 + 2n + 1 = 2(n^2 + n) + 1.",
        "n^2 + n is always a whole number, so 2(n^2 + n) is always even - and the sum of squares is exactly 1 more than that even number, so it is always odd."
      ],
      "final_answer": "Proven: n^2 + (n + 1)^2 = 2(n^2 + n) + 1, always odd.",
      "marks": 5,
